{"kind":"FETCH","data":{"headers":{},"body":"{\"id\":7716967,\"site\":\"lawnsguide\",\"type\":\"pest-control\",\"short_title\":\"get-rid-mole-crickets-warm-season-lawns-naturally\",\"language\":\"en\",\"published_time\":\"2026-07-27 00:31:27\",\"modified_time\":\"2026-07-27 00:31:27\",\"author\":\"Robert Hayes\",\"img\":\"https://s.alicdn.com/@sc02/kf/Aad8c82a8648449f79a888f132d6d0992J.jpg\",\"title\":\"How to Get Rid of Mole Crickets in Warm Season Lawns Naturally\",\"description\":\"Learn how to identify, prevent, and naturally eliminate mole crickets from warm season lawns using proven DIY methods and organic solutions.\",\"url\":\"/pest-control/get-rid-mole-crickets-warm-season-lawns-naturally\",\"body\":\"<h1>How to Get Rid of Mole Crickets in Warm Season Lawns Naturally</h1>\\n\\n<p>If your warm season lawn has suddenly developed spongy patches, irregular mounds of soil, and grass that seems to be dying from the roots up, you may be dealing with a mole cricket infestation. These subterranean pests are among the most destructive insects that target warm season turfgrasses like Bermuda, Zoysia, St. Augustine, Centipede, and Bahia grass. Unlike typical crickets that chirp above ground, mole crickets spend most of their lives beneath the soil surface, tunneling through root systems and feeding on grassroots, shoots, and organic matter.</p>\\n\\n<p>Mole crickets are particularly problematic in the southeastern United States, where warm, humid conditions create the perfect breeding environment. A single female can lay up to 130 eggs in a single season, and the nymphs that emerge can cause significant damage before you even notice their presence. The good news is that you do not need to resort to harsh synthetic chemicals to reclaim your lawn. There are numerous natural and organic strategies that effectively control mole cricket populations while keeping your yard safe for children, pets, and beneficial organisms.</p>\\n\\n<p>In this comprehensive guide, we will walk you through everything you need to know about identifying mole cricket damage, preventing infestations before they start, and deploying proven natural remedies to eliminate these pests from your warm season lawn for good. For more general guidance on maintaining a healthy turf, check out our <a href=\\\"https://lawnsguide.com/warm-season-lawn-care-guide\\\">warm season lawn care guide</a> to build a strong foundation that resists pest invasions.</p>\\n\\n<h2>Identifying Mole Crickets and the Damage They Cause</h2>\\n\\n<p>Before you can effectively treat a mole cricket problem, you need to confirm that mole crickets are actually the culprit. Several lawn pests create similar symptoms, and misidentification can lead to wasted time and ineffective treatments. Understanding what mole crickets look like, how they behave, and the specific patterns of damage they leave behind is the first critical step in natural pest management.</p>\\n\\n<h3>What Do Mole Crickets Look Like?</h3>\\n\\n<p>Mole crickets are easily distinguishable from other cricket species once you know what to look for. Adult mole crickets typically measure between one and two inches in length and have a distinctive cylindrical body shape. Their most notable features are their enlarged, shovel-like front legs that are specially adapted for digging through soil. These powerful forelegs function much like the claws of a mole, which is how the insect earned its common name.</p>\\n\\n<p>Their bodies range in color from light brown to dark grayish-brown, and they have short, leathery wings that fold flat against their backs. Their heads are broad and rounded, with small but visible eyes and long antennae. When you dig one up from the soil, you may notice their velvety texture, which comes from fine hairs covering their bodies. The nymphs look like smaller versions of the adults but lack fully developed wings.</p>\\n\\n<p>There are several species of mole crickets that affect lawns in North America, with the tawny mole cricket, southern mole cricket, and shortwinged mole cricket being the most common culprits in warm season turf areas. The tawny mole cricket tends to be the most destructive, as it feeds primarily on grass roots and shoots rather than other insects.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Signs of Mole Cricket Damage</h3>\\n\\n<p>Mole cricket damage manifests in several distinctive ways that differentiate it from damage caused by grubs, chinch bugs, or fungal diseases. The most obvious sign is the presence of raised, irregular tunnels just beneath the soil surface. These tunnels feel spongy when you walk across them, and the grass above may appear thin, yellowed, or completely dead in streaky patterns.</p>\\n\\n<p>Unlike grub damage, where the grass can be rolled back like a carpet because the roots have been severed, mole cricket damage tends to be more scattered and tunnel-oriented. You will notice small mounds of pushed-up soil scattered across your lawn, resembling miniature versions of mole hills. These mounds typically appear in the early morning after the insects have been tunneling near the surface during the night.</p>\\n\\n<p>Another key indicator is irregular patches of thinning grass that seem to expand over time, particularly during late summer and early fall when nymph populations are at their peak. The grass blades may appear notched or chewed, and if you pull on affected turf, it may lift more easily than healthy grass because the root system has been undermined by tunneling activity.</p>\\n\\n<h3>The Soap Flush Test: Confirming an Infestation</h3>\\n\\n<p>The most reliable way to confirm a mole cricket infestation is to perform a soap flush test. This simple diagnostic technique forces the insects to the surface where you can count them and assess the severity of the problem. To perform the test, mix two tablespoons of liquid dish soap with two gallons of water and pour the solution over a four-square-foot area of suspected infestation.</p>\\n\\n<p>Wait approximately three to five minutes, and watch as mole crickets and other soil-dwelling insects emerge from the ground, irritated by the soapy water. If you see two to four mole crickets surface in that small area, you have a significant enough infestation to warrant treatment. Fewer than two may indicate a manageable population that can be controlled through cultural practices alone. For more on diagnosing lawn problems, visit our <a href=\\\"https://lawnsguide.com/pest-control/common-lawn-pests-identification\\\">common lawn pests identification page</a>.</p>\\n\\n<h2>Preventing Mole Crickets From Invading Your Warm Season Lawn</h2>\\n\\n<p>As with most pest management strategies, prevention is far more effective and less labor-intensive than trying to eliminate an established population. Mole crickets are attracted to specific lawn conditions, and by modifying your maintenance practices, you can make your warm season turf far less inviting to these destructive insects. A healthy, well-maintained lawn is your first and strongest line of defense.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Maintain Proper Mowing Heights</h3>\\n\\n<p>One of the most overlooked factors in mole cricket prevention is mowing height. Warm season grasses that are cut too short become stressed and develop shallow root systems, making them more susceptible to tunneling damage. Additionally, closely mowed lawns provide less shade at the soil surface, creating the warm, exposed conditions that female mole crickets prefer when selecting egg-laying sites.</p>\\n\\n<p>Keep your Bermuda grass between one and two inches, Zoysia between one and two and a half inches, and St. Augustine between two and four inches. These taller mowing heights promote deeper root growth, denser turf canopy, and a cooler soil surface that discourages egg deposition. Always follow the one-third rule when mowing, never removing more than one-third of the grass blade in a single cutting session. Our <a href=\\\"https://lawnsguide.com/lawn-mowing/mowing-heights-warm-season-grasses\\\">mowing heights guide for warm season grasses</a> provides species-specific recommendations.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Manage Thatch Buildup</h3>\\n\\n<p>Excessive thatch, the layer of dead and living organic matter between the grass blades and soil surface, creates an ideal habitat for mole crickets. A thick thatch layer provides shelter, retains moisture near the soil surface, and makes it difficult for natural predators and biological controls to reach the insects. Warm season grasses, particularly Bermuda and Zoysia, are prone to thatch accumulation and require regular monitoring.</p>\\n\\n<p>Check your thatch layer annually by cutting a small wedge of turf with a sharp knife or trowel. If the thatch layer exceeds half an inch in thickness, it is time to dethatch. For warm season lawns, the best time to dethatch is during peak growing season in late spring or early summer, when the grass can recover quickly. Use a vertical mower or power rake for heavy thatch, or a stiff-tined rake for lighter accumulations. Proper <a href=\\\"https://lawnsguide.com/lawn-maintenance/dethatching-warm-season-lawns\\\">dethatching practices</a> not only deter mole crickets but also improve water penetration and nutrient uptake.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Optimize Your Irrigation Practices</h3>\\n\\n<p>Mole crickets thrive in moist soil conditions, and overwatering is one of the most common mistakes that invites infestation. Frequent, shallow irrigation keeps the top layer of soil perpetually damp, creating perfect tunneling conditions and encouraging egg survival. Female mole crickets actively seek out moist soil for egg laying, so reducing surface moisture during peak egg-laying periods in late spring and early summer can significantly reduce recruitment.</p>\\n\\n<p>Transition to a deep and infrequent watering schedule that encourages roots to grow deeper into the soil profile. Water your warm season lawn deeply once or twice per week rather than lightly every day, applying approximately one inch of water per session. Allow the soil surface to dry between waterings. Early morning irrigation is ideal, as it allows the grass blades to dry before nightfall, reducing disease pressure as well. Learn more about efficient watering in our <a href=\\\"https://lawnsguide.com/lawn-watering/watering-warm-season-lawns\\\">warm season lawn watering guide</a>.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Encourage a Dense, Healthy Turf</h3>\\n\\n<p>A thick, vigorously growing lawn is naturally resistant to mole cricket damage because the dense root mass makes tunneling more difficult and the grass can outgrow minor feeding damage. Proper fertilization tailored to your specific warm season grass species promotes the kind of dense growth that deters pests. Soil testing every two to three years ensures you are applying the right nutrients in the right amounts.</p>\\n\\n<p>Avoid excessive nitrogen applications, which can produce lush but weak growth that is actually more attractive to pests. Instead, use balanced, slow-release fertilizers that promote steady, sustainable growth. Overseeding thin areas promptly and addressing bare spots before weeds or pests can exploit them keeps your turf uniformly dense and resilient.</p>\\n\\n<h2>Natural and DIY Solutions to Eliminate Mole Crickets</h2>\\n\\n<p>Once you have confirmed a mole cricket infestation and taken preventive measures, it is time to deploy targeted natural treatments. The following methods have been proven effective against mole crickets in warm season lawns and can be used individually or in combination for maximum impact. Timing is critical with natural controls, so pay close attention to the seasonal application windows described below.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Beneficial Nematodes: Nature's Underground Army</h3>\\n\\n<p>Beneficial nematodes are microscopic, soil-dwelling roundworms that parasitize and kill mole crickets and other soil insects. Specifically, the species <em>Steinernema scapterisci</em> and <em>Steinernema riobrave</em> have demonstrated exceptional effectiveness against mole crickets in university research trials. These nematodes actively seek out their hosts, entering the insect's body through natural openings and releasing bacteria that kill the mole cricket within 24 to 48 hours.</p>\\n\\n<p>Apply beneficial nematodes during late summer or early fall when mole cricket nymphs are small and most vulnerable. The soil temperature should be between 60 and 90 degrees Fahrenheit for optimal nematode activity. Water the lawn thoroughly before application to moisten the soil, then apply the nematodes using a hose-end sprayer or watering can according to the product's instructions. Water again immediately after application to wash the nematodes into the soil where they can begin hunting.</p>\\n\\n<p>Nematodes are living organisms, so purchase them from reputable suppliers who ship them in temperature-controlled packaging and store them in the refrigerator until application day. Avoid applying nematodes during the heat of the day or when the soil is dry, as desiccation will kill them before they can reach their targets. When used correctly, nematodes can reduce mole cricket populations by 70 to 90 percent within a single season.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Neem Oil Applications</h3>\\n\\n<p>Neem oil, extracted from the seeds of the neem tree (<em>Azadirachta indica</em>), is a versatile natural pesticide that disrupts the life cycle of mole crickets at multiple stages. The active compound, azadirachtin, acts as an antifeedant, growth regulator, and repellent. When mole crickets come into contact with or ingest neem oil, their feeding behavior is suppressed, their molting process is disrupted, and their reproductive capacity is diminished.</p>\\n\\n<p>For mole cricket control, mix neem oil concentrate with water according to the product label, typically at a rate of one to two tablespoons per gallon of water. Apply the solution to affected areas using a pump sprayer, targeting the soil surface and the base of the grass where tunnels are visible. Apply in the late evening when mole crickets are most active near the surface and when the solution will not be immediately broken down by sunlight and heat.</p>\\n\\n<p>Repeat applications every seven to fourteen days during active infestation periods. Neem oil is safe for use around pets and children once it has dried, and it does not harm earthworms or most beneficial soil organisms. It also has the added benefit of controlling a wide range of other lawn pests, including chinch bugs, sod webworms, and armyworms. For more on organic pest management, explore our <a href=\\\"https://lawnsguide.com/pest-control/natural-pest-control-methods\\\">natural pest control methods guide</a>.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Diatomaceous Earth</h3>\\n\\n<p>Food-grade diatomaceous earth (DE) is a naturally occurring, fossilized algae deposit that works as a mechanical insecticide. Under a microscope, DE particles appear as sharp, jagged shards that cut through the waxy exoskeleton of insects, causing them to dehydrate and die. When mole crickets tunnel through soil treated with diatomaceous earth or come into contact with it at the surface, the abrasive particles compromise their protective outer layer.</p>\\n\\n<p>Apply diatomaceous earth by dusting it over the soil surface in areas where mole cricket activity has been identified, particularly around tunnel entrances and soil mounds. Use a hand duster or shaker to distribute a thin, even layer. For best results, apply after watering or rainfall and allow it to settle into the upper soil layer. DE must remain dry to be effective, so reapply after heavy rain or irrigation.</p>\\n\\n<p>While DE is most effective as a surface treatment and may not reach deep-burrowing adults, it provides excellent control of nymphs that spend more time near the surface. It is completely non-toxic to humans and pets, making it a safe choice for families who use their lawns for recreation. Wear a dust mask during application to avoid inhaling fine particles.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Beneficial Predators and Biological Controls</h3>\\n\\n<p>Nature provides its own mole cricket management through a variety of predatory organisms that can be encouraged in your landscape. Birds, particularly robins, starlings, and bluebirds, are voracious consumers of mole crickets and will actively forage for them in lawns. Encourage bird activity by providing birdhouses, birdbaths, and native plantings that offer shelter and food sources.</p>\\n\\n<p>Ground beetles, rove beetles, and tiger beetles are beneficial insects that prey on mole cricket eggs and small nymphs. Avoid broad-spectrum insecticides that would kill these helpful predators along with the pests. Maintaining diverse plantings around the borders of your lawn provides habitat for these beneficial insects and ensures a steady population of natural mole cricket hunters.</p>\\n\\n<p>In some regions, parasitic wasps of the genus <em>Larra</em> have been introduced as biological control agents against mole crickets. These wasps, particularly <em>Larra bicolor</em>, sting adult mole crickets and lay eggs on them, with the developing wasp larvae eventually killing the host. Planting nectar sources like <em>Spermacoce verticillata</em> and <em>Pentas lanceolata</em> near your lawn can attract and sustain populations of these beneficial wasps.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Garlic and Pepper Spray Repellents</h3>\\n\\n<p>For a readily available, kitchen-based repellent, a homemade garlic and hot pepper spray can deter mole crickets from treated areas. The strong sulfur compounds in garlic and the capsaicin in hot peppers create an environment that mole crickets find highly unpleasant, encouraging them to relocate away from your lawn.</p>\\n\\n<p>To make this spray, blend six to eight cloves of garlic and two to three hot peppers (such as habanero or cayenne) with a quart of water. Strain the mixture through cheesecloth to remove solid particles, then add a teaspoon of liquid dish soap to help the solution adhere to soil particles and grass blades. Dilute this concentrate with an additional quart of water and apply to affected areas using a pump sprayer.</p>\\n\\n<p>Reapply every five to seven days and after rainfall. While this method is most effective as a deterrent rather than a direct kill treatment, it works well in combination with other strategies and is completely safe for all organisms in your yard. The strong scent may linger for a day or two after application, which is a small trade-off for chemical-free pest management.</p>\\n\\n<h2>Seasonal Treatment Calendar for Mole Cricket Control</h2>\\n\\n<p>Timing is everything when it comes to natural mole cricket management. Understanding the pest's life cycle allows you to intervene at the most vulnerable stages and maximize the effectiveness of your chosen control methods. Mole crickets follow a predictable annual cycle in warm season lawn regions, and aligning your treatments with this cycle dramatically improves your results.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Spring: Monitoring and Early Intervention</h3>\\n\\n<p>In early spring, adult mole crickets that have overwintered deep in the soil begin moving toward the surface as soil temperatures rise above 60 degrees Fahrenheit. This is the time to begin monitoring your lawn using the soap flush test, checking for adult activity in areas that showed damage the previous season. Adult males begin their distinctive tunneling courtship calls, producing a low, buzzing trill that can be heard on warm spring evenings.</p>\\n\\n<p>Apply diatomaceous earth and neem oil treatments during this period to target adults before they begin mating. Female mole crickets typically begin laying eggs in late spring, with peak egg-laying occurring in late May through June in most warm season lawn regions. Trapping adults before they reproduce is one of the most effective strategies for reducing the overall population pressure for the rest of the season.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Summer: Targeting Vulnerable Nymphs</h3>\\n\\n<p>Eggs hatch throughout the summer months, producing tiny, pale nymphs that are far more susceptible to natural controls than adults. This is the optimal window for applying beneficial nematodes, as the small nymphs are easier for the nematodes to parasitize and kill. Continue neem oil applications on a two-week schedule through the summer to disrupt nymph development and feeding.</p>\\n\\n<p>Mid to late summer is when damage typically becomes most visible, as the growing nymph population consumes increasing amounts of root material. Continue soap flush tests every two to three weeks to monitor population levels and adjust treatment intensity accordingly. Keep your lawn well-watered during this period to help it withstand and recover from any feeding damage that does occur.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Fall: Final Push Before Dormancy</h3>\\n\\n<p>By early fall, surviving nymphs have grown to near-adult size and are preparing to burrow deeper into the soil for winter dormancy. This is your last opportunity to reduce the population before the insects become inaccessible. Apply a final round of beneficial nematodes in early fall while soil temperatures are still warm enough for nematode activity.</p>\\n\\n<p>Fall is also an excellent time to focus on lawn recovery and strengthening. <a href=\\\"https://lawnsguide.com/lawn-fertilization/fall-fertilizing-warm-season-lawns\\\">Fall fertilization</a> helps your warm season grass build strong root systems and store energy for the winter, ensuring it emerges vigorously in spring and is better equipped to resist future pest pressure. Overseed any damaged areas and apply a thin layer of compost to improve soil biology and structure.</p>\\n\\n<h2>When to Call a Professional for Mole Cricket Control</h2>\\n\\n<p>While natural methods are effective for most residential mole cricket infestations, there are situations where professional intervention becomes necessary. Recognizing these thresholds helps you avoid wasting time on treatments that are insufficient for the scale of your problem.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Signs That DIY Methods Are Not Working</h3>\\n\\n<p>If you have been consistently applying natural treatments for six to eight weeks and soap flush tests continue to reveal four or more mole crickets per four-square-foot area, your infestation may be too severe for DIY methods alone. Extensive lawn damage covering more than 30 percent of your turf area, rapid expansion of damaged zones despite treatment, and recurring infestations year after year are all indicators that professional-grade solutions may be needed.</p>\\n\\n<p>Large properties, particularly those exceeding half an acre, may also benefit from professional treatment because achieving thorough coverage with consumer-grade equipment and products is challenging at that scale. Commercial lawn care companies have access to specialized application equipment and professional-grade biological products that can treat large areas more efficiently and effectively.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Choosing a Pest Control Professional</h3>\\n\\n<p>If you decide to hire a professional, look for companies that offer integrated pest management (IPM) approaches rather than those that default to synthetic chemical treatments. A quality pest control provider will perform thorough inspections, use monitoring techniques to assess population levels, and recommend a combination of cultural, biological, and targeted chemical controls when absolutely necessary.</p>\\n\\n<p>Ask potential providers about their experience specifically with mole cricket management in warm season lawns, and request references from customers who have dealt with similar infestations. Ensure they are licensed and insured, and ask about the specific products they plan to use and their environmental impact. Many professional services now offer organic or reduced-risk treatment programs that align with natural lawn care philosophies.</p>\\n\\n<p>For additional support with other turf pests that may accompany or be confused with mole cricket damage, review our guides on <a href=\\\"https://lawnsguide.com/pest-control/get-rid-of-grubs-naturally\\\">getting rid of grubs naturally</a> and <a href=\\\"https://lawnsguide.com/pest-control/chinch-bug-control-warm-season-lawns\\\">chinch bug control for warm season lawns</a>.</p>\\n\\n<h2>Frequently Asked Questions About Mole Cricket Control</h2>\\n\\n<h3>How Do I Know If Mole Crickets Are Causing My Lawn Damage and Not Moles or Grubs?</h3>\\n\\n<p>Mole crickets, moles, and grubs each leave distinct signatures in your lawn. Mole cricket tunnels are narrow, typically about half an inch wide, and run just below the surface with small soil mounds pushed up at intervals. Mole tunnels are considerably wider, usually two to three inches across, and create raised ridges that are much more prominent. Mole hills are also significantly larger, often six inches or more in diameter.</p>\\n\\n<p>Grub damage, by contrast, does not produce tunnels or soil mounds. Instead, grubs sever grass roots, causing the turf to detach from the soil entirely. You can grab a handful of grub-damaged grass and pull it up like a piece of loose carpet, revealing C-shaped white larvae in the soil beneath. The soap flush test will confirm mole crickets, while digging up a square foot of damaged turf will reveal grubs if they are present.</p>\\n\\n<h3>What Time of Year Is Best to Treat for Mole Crickets Naturally?</h3>\\n\\n<p>The most effective treatment window for natural mole cricket control is mid-summer through early fall, when newly hatched nymphs are small and most vulnerable to biological controls like beneficial nematodes and neem oil. During this period, the nymphs are actively feeding near the soil surface, making them accessible to treatments that would not reach deeper-burrowing adults.</p>\\n\\n<p>Secondary treatment should occur in spring when adults are active near the surface and before egg laying peaks. Combining spring adult suppression with summer nymph targeting provides the most comprehensive season-long control. Avoid treating during winter months when mole crickets are dormant deep in the soil, as treatments will have minimal contact with the insects.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Are Mole Crickets Harmful to Humans or Pets?</h3>\\n\\n<p>Mole crickets pose no direct threat to humans or pets. They do not bite, sting, or transmit diseases, and they are not known to carry parasites that affect mammals. Their damage is purely to turfgrass and other plants. In fact, mole crickets are sometimes considered a sign of healthy, biodiverse soil in agricultural contexts, though their tunneling activity is unwelcome in managed lawns.</p>\\n\\n<p>The primary concern with mole crickets is the indirect damage to your lawn. Severe infestations can kill large areas of turf, creating bare spots that invite weed invasion, soil erosion, and mud problems. The spongy, uneven surface created by tunneling can also be a tripping hazard during active play or sports on the lawn.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Can Mole Crickets Fly, and Will They Come Back After Treatment?</h3>\\n\\n<p>Yes, adult mole crickets of most species can fly, and they typically do so during mating season in spring and early summer. Males produce calling songs from their tunnels to attract flying females, and mated females may fly to new locations to lay eggs. This flight capability means that even if you successfully eliminate the population in your lawn, reinfestation from neighboring properties is always possible.</p>\\n\\n<p>This is why ongoing prevention measures are so important. Maintaining a dense, healthy lawn with proper mowing heights, managed thatch levels, and appropriate irrigation practices creates conditions that are inherently less attractive to incoming mole crickets. Regular monitoring with soap flush tests allows you to detect new arrivals early, before populations can build to damaging levels. Consider coordinating with neighbors on mole cricket management, as area-wide control efforts are significantly more effective than individual property treatments.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Will Beneficial Nematodes Harm My Lawn, Garden Plants, or Earthworms?</h3>\\n\\n<p>Beneficial nematodes used for mole cricket control are completely safe for lawns, garden plants, earthworms, and all other non-target organisms. These nematodes are highly specific in their host preferences, targeting only certain groups of insects such as mole crickets, grubs, and other soil-dwelling pests. They have been extensively studied and approved for use in organic agriculture and residential landscapes.</p>\\n\\n<p>Earthworms, which are crucial for soil health, are not affected by the nematode species used for mole cricket control. In fact, by reducing the need for chemical insecticides, using nematodes indirectly benefits earthworm populations. The nematodes also break down naturally in the soil within a few weeks if they do not find suitable hosts, leaving no residual environmental impact. They are one of the safest and most environmentally responsible pest control tools available to homeowners.</p>\",\"tag\":null,\"is_online\":\"Y\",\"subtype\":null,\"slug\":null,\"cover_image\":null,\"status\":\"published\",\"created_at\":\"2026-07-27 00:35:21\"}","status":200,"url":""},"revalidate":3600,"tags":[]}